body: |-
  SETUP: test/false-autofold-gate.test.ts clones the FixtureRepo harness (throwaway git repo with .ts files), defines adversarial cross-symbol semantic-conflict pairs + controls, runs consolidate([branchA,branchB]) on each, and classifies dangle/knot/autofold.

  THE FINDING (mechanistic, not just a rate): essence is Merkle-by-target — a caller's essence inlines its callees' essences. So when conflicting symbols are dependency-LINKED in the lifted graph (one calls/uses the other, or both feed a common in-graph dependent), an upstream change propagates into the dependent's essence; both branches then move that dependent to different essences → KNOT → CAUGHT. When the conflicting symbols are INDEPENDENT (no in-graph edge, composed only externally) or composed across an IMPORT boundary (cross-file refs are EXTERN free-refs, never local code-unit edges → no Merkle link), there is no propagation → autoClean → silent false-CLEAN.

  THE KILLER CONTRAST: the SAME double-tax bug (rate*1.08 AND applyTax*1.08 → 1.1664) is CAUGHT same-file (applyTax CALLS rate) but BLIND cross-file (applyTax IMPORTS rate). Identical semantic bug, opposite engine verdict, decided purely by whether the link is a local edge or an extern ref.

  NUMBERS (this corpus, 8 adversarial + 3 controls): dependency-linked 3/3 caught (double-tax-samefile, double-discount-samefile, nullable-drift-samefile); unlinked 5/5 blind (double-tax-crossfile, roundtrip-broken, bounds-inverted, version-skew, units-ms-vs-sec); overall false-CLEAN 5/8 = 63%. Controls: same-symbol knot caught, code dangle caught, genuinely-independent edits correctly clean.

  IMPLICATION FOR PHASE C (multi-writer admission): auto-admit a WEFT ONLY when its conflict surface is dependency-connected in the lifted graph; a WEFT spanning independent symbols or cross-file/extern refs MUST require human/peer confirmation before admission. This makes the admission gate a graph-reachability question (is the contested surface within one dependency cone?), which is computable from the existing edge graph. Two engine improvements would shrink the blind spot: (1) lift cross-file imports to RESOLVED local edges (not extern by specifier) so the Merkle link survives file boundaries — but that costs determinism (needs node_modules/checker resolution); (2) a whole-program invariant layer above per-symbol essence. Both are out of scope now; the confirm-gate is the safe v1.

  This is the moat-relevant outcome: Warpline can HONESTLY say where it can auto-merge safely (dependency-connected) vs where it must defer to a human — which is exactly the calibrated, legible behavior a meaning-native VCS needs to earn trust.</parameter>
